  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry weather will persist for most of the \nupcoming week. Below normal temperatures with frost and/or \nfreeze conditions are expected each night and early morning \nthrough the middle of the week.\n\nThe persistent mid/upper ridge over the eastern U.S. will \ngradually deamplify as a ridge builds eastward. There are \nvariations in the upper pattern by mid-week and subsequently the\nhandling of low pressure off the southeast U.S. coast. \nCurrently feel that the operational GFS is an outlier with a \nmore reasonable solution keeping any low pressure offshore with \nthe surface ridge holding across the eastern U.S. As a result, \nexpect the column to remain dry through Friday. Additionally, \nthe airmass will only gradually moderate with the potential for \nfreezing temperatures/frost each early morning through \nWednesday. The potential for frost will exist, but expect it \nwill be patchy along the coast so no advisory is needed at this \ntime. There will be no Frost Advisories or Freeze Warnings for \nthose areas where the growing season has ended. The only \nexceptions moving forward through Dec 1 will be coastal Pender, \nNew Hanover, coastal Brunswick, coastal Horry, and coastal \nGeorgetown. Expect to see temperatures climb back above normal \nThursday through Saturday. Could see some rain chances by later \nSaturday.",
